    S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:seteq"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:dedup"), N(1)), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:dedup"), N(2))))),

DEF("test:names",
    S2(A("ALPHA"), N(1))),

DEF("test:fix_table",
    S3(A("CONS"), N(1), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:unfold_atoms"), N(2)))),

DEF("test:norma",
    S4(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), A("test:fix_table")), A("theta:unfold_pairs"), S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:fetch"), S3(A("CONS"), S2(A("CONST"), A("norma:tables")), A("id"))))),

"A STORE CLOSED UNDER ITS OWN RULES. Figure 1 is resolve -> lfp -> validate -> emit, so a store that has never had its fixpoint taken is not at one: over the composed order store this gains 26 populations that are derivable and absent, the whole state-machine family among them. It is needed in two places for the same reason -- at load, because a store that is not closed is not the store, and after a create, because adding a fact a rule READS leaves every consequence of it stale. Measured: creating TransitionIsFromStatus <t-new, zzznew> lands the fact and leaves StatusIsDefinedInStateMachineDefinition without zzznew.",
"THE SHAPES ARE THE ONES test:apply USES. store:fts applies store:fix_desc so column 5 is the rows themselves; induce:pairs_of then takes columns 1 and 5; and it is rules:metamodel, because rules:model cannot derive StatusIsDefinedInStateMachineDefinition, a population its own minus rules read. Derived cells go in FRONT: a descriptor walk takes the first, and the derived population is the fresher one.",
"IS THE STORE CLOSED UNDER ITS OWN RULES? Nothing asked. test:derive is named derive-fixpoint and tests the fixpoint OPERATOR over a literal fixture -- contained <a,b>,<b,c>,<c,d> and its transitive closure -- which is a correct law and not this one. So 26 derivable populations, the entire state-machine family among them, were absent from the composed store while all 53 laws held. A store that is not closed is not the store, and this is the law that says so: every population the rules derive must already be answerable from D. Compare by NAME and emptiness rather than by rows, because a derived population that is present and stale is a different failure and the rows comparison would report it here instead of where it belongs.",
DEF("test:closed_nz",
    S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), S3(A("COMP"), N(2), N(1))),
       N(2),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("apndl"), S3(A("CONS"), N(1), N(2))))),
DEF("test:closed_rows",
    S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("INSERT"), A("test:closed_nz")), A("theta:append_phi"))),
"AND A POPULATION PRESENT BUT STALE IS THE FAILURE THIS ONE NOW CATCHES. The names comparison could not: with StatusIsDefinedInStateMachineDefinition present and out of date, it said T. It compares rows, and it costs less than the version that did not -- 0.6s against 1.0s, because seteq stops early where null had to build the whole answer.",
"THE TWO PATHS AGREE NOW, so this compares rows plainly. They did not: derive answered a unary population as one-tuples and the accessor as bare atoms, same 29 rows for OperationIsOverridable and 7 for OperationIsRegistrable, and test:closed_norm wrapped both sides so the comparison could proceed. The wrapper is gone with the disagreement -- a unary fact is a one-tuple everywhere, and the accessor is what changed, because FILE stores a one-column table as a list of values and a value is not a fact.",
"THE CLOSURE IS TAKEN UNDER THE WHOLE PROGRAM. Stratification is over derive:all_rules because a negation cycle can span rules:metamodel and rules:model with neither half showing it; a store closed under only 39 of its 60 rules is not closed for the same reason. Measured: on both the oracle and the order carriers the extra 21 produce NOTHING -- 37 and 38 populations either way, none of them only from the model rules, because those heads have empty inputs. So this asserts strictly more and costs nothing, 91ms either way, which is the moment to make the claim rather than after an app rule has consequences no one checked.",
DEF("test:closed_pairs",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("test:closed_rows"), A("derive:closed"))),
DEF("test:closed_missing",
    S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("INSERT"), A("test:closed_keep")), A("theta:append_phi"))),
DEF("test:closed_keep",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:seteq"),
          S3(A("CONS"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("system:pop_rows"),
                S3(A("CONS"), S4(A("COMP"), N(1), N(1), N(1)), S3(A("COMP"), N(2), N(1)))),
             S4(A("COMP"), N(2), N(1), N(1)))),
       N(2),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("apndl"), S3(A("CONS"), S4(A("COMP"), N(1), N(1), N(1)), N(2))))),
DEF("test:closed",
    S5(A("COMP"), A("null"), A("test:closed_missing"), A("distr"),
       S3(A("CONS"), A("test:closed_pairs"), A("id")))),
"DERIVE ON READ AGREES WITH THE CLOSURE. For every head a store computes on read, derive:on_read answers the rows the closure answers for it: derive:closed over the whole store, which starts every head its rules own empty. A read of such a head is derive:on_read itself (ast:FetchPop), so the law holds the computation to the closure and not to what a read answers, which would be the computation again.",
DEF("test:on_read",
    S5(A("COMP"), A("fp:and_all"), S2(A("ALPHA"), A("test:or_one")), A("distr"), S3(A("CONS"), A("derive:or_heads"), A("id")))),
"<head, store> to T when the head`s computed rows are the rows the store`s closure answers for it.",
DEF("test:or_one",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("test:rows_eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("derive:on_read"), S3(A("COMP"), A("derive:pop"), S3(A("CONS"), N(1), S3(A("COMP"), A("derive:closed"), N(2))))))),
DEF("test:tenant_local",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("tenant:own"), A("tenant:fetched")))),
DEF("test:tenant_iso",
    S5(A("COMP"), A("fp:and_all"), S2(A("ALPHA"), A("test:tenant_local")),
       S2(A("ALPHA"), A("ast:fetch")),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("distr"), S3(A("CONS"), A("tenant:names"), A("id"))))),
"test:perform_params holds perform:params_of over the composed store with a Function law-fn that has Query Parameter depth with Parameter Value 0 and sends UserHasEmail`s second role to Query Parameter where[email][equals], and a User law-user whose Email is law@example.com: it answers depth 0, then where[email][equals] law@example.com.",
DEF("test:pp_store",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("test:sp_put"), S4(A("CONS"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("test:sp_put"), S4(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("test:sp_put"), S4(A("CONS"), A("id"), K(A("FunctionHasQueryParameterWithParameterValue")), K(S3(A("law-fn"), A("depth"), A("0"))))),
          K(A("FunctionSendsFactTypeWithRoleToQueryParameter")), K(S4(A("law-fn"), A("UserHasEmail"), A("UserHasEmail.2"), A("where[email][equals]"))))),
       K(A("UserHasEmail")), K(S2(A("law-user"), A("law@example.com")))))),
DEF("test:perform_params",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("perform:params_of"), S4(A("CONS"), K(A("law-fn")), K(A("law-user")), A("test:pp_store"))),
       K(S2(S2(A("depth"), A("0")), S2(A("where[email][equals]"), A("law@example.com"))))))),
DEF("test:log_resolve",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("log:resolve_with"), K(S3(A("lawlog"), A("write"), S2(A("response"), S2(A("f"), A("200")))))),
       K(S2(A("response"), S2(A("f"), A("200"))))))),
"test:ot_undo holds fed:ot_undo over test:ot_store: a failed UserIsHuman:set of a@x is undone by retracting UserIsHuman a@x, a failed :unset by asserting it, a failed UserHasAccessRole:set by retracting the role the store holds, and a predicate that is no federated write is undone by nothing.",
DEF("test:ot_undo",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("fp:and_all"), S5(A("CONS"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("fed:ot_undo"), S4(A("CONS"), K(A("UserIsHuman:set")), K(A("a@x")), A("test:ot_store"))),
          K(S2(A("DELETE"), S2(A("UserIsHuman"), A("a@x")))))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("fed:ot_undo"), S4(A("CONS"), K(A("UserIsHuman:unset")), K(A("a@x")), A("test:ot_store"))),
          K(S2(A("POST"), S2(A("UserIsHuman"), A("a@x")))))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("fed:ot_undo"), S4(A("CONS"), K(A("UserHasAccessRole:set")), K(A("a@x")),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("test:sp_put"), S4(A("CONS"), A("test:ot_store"), K(A("UserHasAccessRole")), K(S2(A("a@x"), A("admin"))))))),
          K(S2(A("DELETE"), S3(A("UserHasAccessRole"), A("a@x"), A("admin")))))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("null"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("fed:ot_undo"), S4(A("CONS"), K(A("sendSupportEmail")), K(A("a@x")), A("test:ot_store"))))))),
"test:response_facts holds a response of User from /api/users, 200, at 2026-10-06T00:00:00Z, to its four facts under response-User-2026-10-06T00:00:00Z.",
DEF("test:response_facts",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("fed:response_facts"), K(S4(A("User"), A("https://auth.vin/api/users"), A("200"), A("2026-10-06T00:00:00Z")))),
       K(S4(S3(A("ResponseAnswersFunction"), A("response-User-2026-10-06T00:00:00Z"), A("User")),
            S3(A("ResponseIsFromUrl"), A("response-User-2026-10-06T00:00:00Z"), A("https://auth.vin/api/users")),
            S3(A("ResponseHasHTTPStatusCode"), A("response-User-2026-10-06T00:00:00Z"), A("200")),
            S3(A("ResponseOccurredAtTimestamp"), A("response-User-2026-10-06T00:00:00Z"), A("2026-10-06T00:00:00Z"))))))),
"AND THE RESPONSE IS RECORDED, NOT ONLY SPELLED. test:response_facts held the four facts and nothing asserted them: on support.auto.dev a keyed sync of User went off the line and answered, and ResponseAnswersFunction stayed empty, because the host asks main:as to assert them and drops a refusal without a word. This asserts one Response over the composed store as the host does, by the system login, and holds that the write commits.",
DEF("test:response_commits",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(2), S3(A("COMP"), A("main:as"), S4(A("CONS"), A("id"), K(A("cicd@repo.do")), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("assert")), S3(A("COMP"), A("fed:response_facts"), K(S4(A("Domain"), A("https://auth.vin/api/users"), A("200"), A("2026-10-06T00:00:00Z")))))))), K(A("T"))))),
"test:ot_roundtrip holds fed:ot_roundtrip_bad over test:ot_store, which declares auth.vin`s role read through lower and written through upper: with a User whose role is admin nothing is answered, and with the role declared written through quoted -- which no read through lower undoes -- the value is answered.",
DEF("test:ot_roundtrip",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("and"), S3(A("CONS"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), S3(A("COMP"), A("fed:ot_roundtrip_bad"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("test:sp_put"), S4(A("CONS"), A("test:ot_store"), K(A("UserHasAccessRole")), K(S2(A("a@x"), A("admin"))))))),
       S4(A("COMP"), A("not"), A("null"), S3(A("COMP"), A("fed:ot_roundtrip_bad"),
