DEF("test:writable",
    S4(A("COMP"), A("null"), A("main:alethic_of"), A("ui:violations"))),
"AND A LAW THAT STOPS EVERY WRITE SHOULD SAY WHAT STOPPED IT. writable answers T or F and nothing else, and F means Thm 1 refuses every successor -- a TOTAL write outage, not a degraded path. The case: a transition is removed from its machine, and readings/ui.md goes on saying `Transition 'redraft' has Event Label 'Redraft'`. That sentence MINTS the Transition, which then has no machine, no from, no to and no trigger -- four unfilled mandatories, four alethic violations, and every POST answering 409. The oracle's check reports 0 errors, correctly: it validates the MODEL and an instance with unfilled mandatory roles is a POPULATION fact. This law is the one that sees it, and its witness answers the two lists a reader needs -- the SUBJECTS that make the store unwritable, then the FACT TYPES whose mandatory roles they leave unfilled -- so `witness` prints `WITNESS writable: redraft, TransitionIsDefinedInStateMachineDefinition, TransitionIsFromStatus, ...` instead of nothing. The law's own verdict is untouched: only the witness is added, so no report and no golden moves.",
"AND THE REAL LESSON IS ABOUT WHICH GATE AN AUTHOR RUNS. The law would have caught this the moment it was written; nobody ran the law report between the edit and the write that failed, because the gate you run after editing readings is `bun run check`, and that gate cannot see a population. The UI facts also live in a DIFFERENT FILE from the declaration, so removing the declaration left ui.md internally consistent and the oracle happy to mint the subject. Grep the name across every reading before deleting a transition, a status or an event type -- and read the witness, which is now worth reading.",
DEF("test:writable_witness",
    S3(A("CONS"),
       S4(A("COMP"), A("theta:dedup"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(3)),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("main:alethic_of"), A("ui:violations"))),
       S4(A("COMP"), A("theta:dedup"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(1)),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("main:alethic_of"), A("ui:violations"))))),
DEF("test:fip_desc",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:subset"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:unfold_rows"), S3(A("COMP"), N(5), N(2))),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("system:pop_rows"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(2)), N(1)))))),
"AND FILE HOLDS THE POPULATION, NOT ONLY THE SOURCE. This law held each descriptor`s rows within the population system:pop_rows reads, and never read FILE, which a GET`s links are made from (nav:peers) and which system:pop_in reads where a fact type has no cell of its own. So a population put by name and never refiled went unseen, and a write`s closure left FILE`s cell of every head it moved as it was. test:fip_cell holds FILE`s cell of a declared fact type, unnested, to be the rows system:pop_rows reads, as a set; a fact type FILE has no cell for reads as none. On this alone the law answered F, and its witness named 29 declared fact types of the composed base store, each a population the start`s reflection or closure had put. Operand <store, descriptor>.",
"AND A HEAD THE STORE COMPUTES ON READ HAS NOTHING IN FILE: FILE holds what the store keeps, and such a head is kept nowhere, so its cell is absent or empty, while system:pop_rows computes it.",
