    S5(A("COMP"), S2(A("INSERT"), A("test:reach_keep")), A("theta:append_phi"),
       S2(A("ALPHA"), N(2)), A("test:lambda_cells"))),
"THE RECORD IS NOT AN EDGE. Naming the 152 unreachable cells in lambda made every one of them reachable FROM the naming, and the count fell to zero: a list of what nothing reaches, read as references, reaches all of it. So the cell holding that list contributes no edges. This is the only exclusion and it is not a convenience -- test:unreach_expected is a statement ABOUT the graph, and letting it be part of the graph means the record changes what it records.",
DEF("test:adj_one",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"),
          S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(2), N(1)), K(A("test:unreach_expected")))),
       S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(2), N(1)), K(PHI())),
       S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(2), N(1)),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("test:refs_of"),
             S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(3), N(1)), N(2)))))),
DEF("test:adj",
    S4(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), A("test:adj_one")), A("distr"),
       S3(A("CONS"), A("test:lambda_cells"), A("test:reach_names")))),
"THE ENTRY POINTS ARE THE FLEET, NOT ONE HOST. I first wrote this list from the js host alone and called it every Ev the host makes, which made 61 ui: cells look unreached; they are entered by the GUI hosts, which ask lambda for ui:boot, ui:screen, ui:arrange, ui:pane_view, ui:iteminner, ui:navpe, ui:render and ui:style, and SUPPLY render:canvas, render:headerbar and the rest natively -- those are iFactr controls each platform draws itself, not defs lambda is missing. tools carries seven runners; a reachability claim scoped to one of them reads dead code that another host runs every time it paints a screen. Cases are entries too, since main dispatches a case by a name built at runtime and no static edge reaches them. store:append is listed because the hosts ask for it, and it has no cell here on purpose: it is a REGISTERED function, the platform binding AREST.tex describes at eq:boundary, sitting beside render:canvas and clock in the twelve the web host registers. It appends to the journal, which is a write outside D, so lambda cannot define it and should not -- a registered definition enters DEFS in the host that registers it, at runtime. The js host does not register it and does not need to, having no journal path; asking it there is an unresolved atom, correctly.",
"DOES LAMBDA OWE THIS FUNCTION? I answered it wrong twice in one night and in both directions -- that lambda owed the eight iFactr renderers, and that it owed store:append -- by reading host source both times. It is a query. Eq 5 filters DEFS on origin = registered and Corollary 7 identifies that restriction with the decidability frontier, so the boundary IS this question, and the law is that restriction asked rather than described in a note. A name the fleet asks for is owed when lambda has no cell for it AND no host has registered it; a registered function is platform binding and owed by nobody.",
DEF("test:owed_cell",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("not"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"),
          S3(A("CONS"), A("ast:fetch"), K(A("#")))))),
DEF("test:owed_reg",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("CONS"), N(1), K(A("registered"))),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("system:pop_rows"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("FunctionHasDefinitionOrigin")), N(2)))))),
DEF("test:owed_keep",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("or"),
          S3(A("CONS"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("test:owed_cell"), N(1)),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("test:owed_reg"), N(1)))),
       N(2),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("apndl"),
          S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(1)), N(2))))),
DEF("test:owed",
    S5(A("COMP"), S2(A("INSERT"), A("test:owed_keep")), A("theta:append_phi"), A("distr"),
       S3(A("CONS"), A("test:entry_hosts"), A("id")))),
DEF("test:owes_nothing",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), A("test:owed"))),
"WHERE A RETIRED NAME WENT, and the two arms that make the answer worth asking for. Function is superseded by Function is one ring over the one id space -- Object Type, Fact Type, Reading and a host registration all identify through Function(.id) -- and this law is the half NORMA cannot state. The parsed ring fragment carries irreflexive, asymmetric and transitive; acyclicity has no spelling, and the property that matters is stronger than acyclicity anyway. FIRST ARM: no retired name still resolves. ast:fetch answers # for a name lambda does not define and a host registration shows up as a Definition Origin row, so a name failing both means nothing -- which is exactly the claim a supersession makes and exactly what a stale memory violates. The failure this was written for is in this repo: finiteness_check was reasoned from long after the fat hosts carrying it were deleted, and the unreachable count was quoted as 36 when the store said 95. SECOND ARM: the retired and current role sets do not meet, so every chain is one hop. A memory that answers superseded-by-X and then makes you ask again about X has spent the lookup it was there to save; when a successor is itself replaced the row is retracted and reasserted, which is an operation this system now has. Together the arms give acyclicity for free: no name is in both roles, so the graph has depth one and cannot close a cycle of any length.",
DEF("test:sup_rows",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("system:pop_rows"),
       S3(A("CONS"), K(A("FunctionIsSupersededByFunction")), A("id")))),
DEF("test:sup_origins",
    S4(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(1)), A("system:pop_rows"),
       S3(A("CONS"), K(A("FunctionHasDefinitionOrigin")), A("id")))),
DEF("test:sup_hasorigin",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"),
       S3(A("CONS"), N(1), S3(A("COMP"), A("test:sup_origins"), N(2))))),
DEF("test:sup_live_keep",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("or"),
          S3(A("CONS"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("test:owed_cell"), N(1)),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("test:sup_hasorigin"), N(1)))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("apndl"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(1)), N(2))),
       N(2))),
DEF("test:sup_stale",
    S5(A("COMP"), S2(A("INSERT"), A("test:sup_live_keep")), A("theta:append_phi"), A("distr"),
       S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(1)), A("test:sup_rows")), A("id")))),
DEF("test:sup_meet_keep",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"),
          S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(1)), S3(A("COMP"), N(2), N(1)))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("apndl"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(1)), N(2))),
       N(2))),
DEF("test:sup_meet",
    S5(A("COMP"), S2(A("INSERT"), A("test:sup_meet_keep")), A("theta:append_phi"), A("distr"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(1)), A("test:sup_rows")),
          S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(2)), A("test:sup_rows"))))),
DEF("test:supersession",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("and"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), A("test:sup_stale")),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), A("test:sup_meet"))))),
"AND THE PAGE HOST`S ENTRY IS AN ENTRY. tools/js-runner/page.js, the host an artifact page runs, applies main for the page it writes and artifact:follow for each link the page follows, so artifact:follow is listed with the other hosts` entries and what it calls, artifact:writes and ui:write, is reached from it.",
