"A LAW THAT CHECKS ONE PROJECTION OF AN EQUATION IS NOT A CHECK OF THE EQUATION. test:thm2 held test:thm2_status over the derived transition rows and nothing else, so it said T while main:links_at answered [] for an entity with three peers -- the transitions leg was right and the ANSWER was half. That projection is now test:thm2_proj, one conjunct of two, and the second holds Eq 5 itself over the representation the API emits: for every address the store really has, main:links_at <store, noun, <id>> equals nav(e) UNION transitions(status(e)) as a SET, the two legs computed apart from it. The universe is Corollary 3's own entry points -- the rank-0 collections, each descended to its entities, which is the corollary's `a collection descends to its entities, an entity's links(e) descends further` -- so the two claims are checked over one walk. Remove nav from main:links_at and this reports F on the first entity with a peer; on the composed store that is 293 addresses, every one of them with a peer.",
DEF("test:thm2_addr",
    S4(A("CONS"), N(1), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(2)), S2(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(2), N(2))))),
