       S3(A("COMP"), A("distl"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("ui:top"))))),
DEF("test:thm2_union",
    S5(A("COMP"), A("fp:and_all"), S2(A("ALPHA"), A("test:thm2_at")), A("distl"),
       S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("test:thm2_addrs")))),
DEF("test:thm2",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("fp:and_all"), S3(A("CONS"), A("test:thm2_proj"), A("test:thm2_union")))),

"COROLLARY 3 (cor:hier, THE VIEW HIERARCHY) WAS BUILT IN FULL AND WATCHED BY NOTHING. ui:hier, ui:rank, ui:sub, ui:top and ui:schemascreen each had exactly one caller and not one of them was a law, so the computation the corollary calls `the whole view hierarchy ... with no hand-written map` could be edited into nonsense and every gate would pass. The corollary makes three statements and this holds all three. RANKED: iterated removal of parentless entity types ranks EVERY type, so the names ui:rank answers are the groups, each exactly once -- a type dropped from the ranking is a view with no place in the hierarchy. TOP: `the initial representation is the collections of rank 0`, so the rank-0 names are exactly ui:top, the groups that are nobody's child. EDGES: removal takes a type only once its parents are gone, so every child relation edge runs from a rank to a rank no higher, `types on a cycle of the child relation sharing the rank at which removal stalls` being the equal case -- measured on the support store, 49 of 127 edges strict and 78 equal, the stall bucket. What the corollary says beyond this -- that a collection descends to its entities and an entity descends by links(e) -- is held in test:thm2_union, which walks exactly that descent.",
DEF("test:cor3_names",
    S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(2)), A("ui:rank"))),
DEF("test:cor3_ranked",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("and"),
