"AND THE RECORDED ANSWER MOVED BECAUSE THE VERB WAS WRONG. get's peer leg recorded <parent, <a>> alone. That was the answer the DEFECT produced, not the answer Eq 5 asks for: nav(e) examines EVERY declared fact type in which e plays a role, and `a` plays one in PersonHasAge as surely as in parent. The old operand -- the rmap group cells, where PersonHasAge is absorbed as a column of Person and its name is nowhere to match -- could only ever find the compound-key fact type that survived as a cell of its own. Reading the FILE population finds both, so the row is now <<PersonHasAge, <a>>, <parent, <a>>>. A recorded answer that agrees with the code is worth nothing when the code is what is in question; this one is re-recorded because the measurement changed, and the measurement changed because the operand did.",
"AND IT MOVED AGAIN WITH THE VIEW. get answers the entity`s facts and links now (the note above DEF(get)), so the row is recorded again over the same two fact types. PersonHasAge is functional on Person, so 33 is a fact of a, named by its fact type, and no link repeats it; parent is spanning, so it is a link at role 1, its address <a>, one row, the other player b. The peer leg`s <<PersonHasAge, <a>>, <parent, <a>>> said only that a plays a role in both, and the slot <<33>> said nothing of which fact type held it.",
"AND A VALUE THAT SPELLS THE ID IS NO LINK. The state get is handed names the entity types now, here Person, and b`s nickname is the value 'a': Nickname is a value type, so a plays no role in PersonHasNickname and the answer is the one recorded above. Before, get kept any row holding the id among its values and answered a third entry, the link <PersonHasNickname, <'', a>, 1, <b>>.",
