    S4(A("COMP"), A("fp:and_all"), S2(A("ALPHA"), S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), S3(A("COMP"), A("cat"), N(2))), N(3)))), A("test:witnesses"))),
"THE REGRESSION CHECK IS A LAW OVER TWO CARRIERS. The oracle writes a run's outcome into design-state (state:built, the built rule heads with multiplicity, sorted; state:errors, the blocking-error total; state:readback, the read-back count) and the same three under expect: names into its expectation file; an accepted run's expectation, composed back in as the carrier `expected`, is what the next run is held to. A store with no expectation is held to nothing, so each law holds of it. `regress` reports the three laws (test:regress_report) and then the heads that differ, lost before new (a head whose multiplicity changed fails the law without appearing there).",

DEF("test:regress_recorded",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("not"), S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:fetch"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("expect:built")), A("id"))), K(A("#")))))),

DEF("test:regress_heads",
    S4(A("COND"), A("atom"), K(PHI()), A("theta:flatten"))),

DEF("test:regress_run",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("test:regress_heads"), S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:fetch"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("state:built")), A("id"))))),

DEF("test:regress_expected",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("test:regress_heads"), S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:fetch"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("expect:built")), A("id"))))),

DEF("test:regress_built",
    S4(A("COND"), A("test:regress_recorded"), S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("test:regress_run"), A("test:regress_expected"))), K(A("T")))),

DEF("test:regress_errors",
    S4(A("COND"), A("test:regress_recorded"), S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:fetch"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("state:errors")), A("id"))), S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:fetch"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("expect:errors")), A("id"))))), K(A("T")))),

DEF("test:regress_readback",
    S4(A("COND"), A("test:regress_recorded"), S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:fetch"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("state:readback")), A("id"))), S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:fetch"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("expect:readback")), A("id"))))), K(A("T")))),

"AND THE CARRIERS THEMSELVES, which is the one this set was missing. The three above are surfaces of the RUN -- which rule heads built, how many errors, how many read-back mismatches -- and what the closure actually consumes is the carriers. So a carrier could change, or stop being written, with every corpus record still reading as recorded: state:rules can take a malformed recipe that crashes every us-law boot, or state:deontics begin carrying constraints it never carried, and all six corpora pass unchanged through either. Each row is one DEF of the schema with its size and a digest, so a record diff says WHICH carrier moved and by how much rather than only that something did. An older record with no expect:carriers holds, as test:regress_recorded already lets an unrecorded run hold: this asks a question of records that answer it.",
DEF("test:regress_carriers",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("not"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"),
             S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:fetch"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("expect:carriers")), A("id"))), K(A("#"))))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"),
          S3(A("CONS"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:fetch"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("state:carriers")), A("id"))),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:fetch"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("expect:carriers")), A("id"))))),
       K(A("T")))),

DEF("test:regress_witness",
    S4(A("COND"), A("test:regress_recorded"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:setminus"), S3(A("CONS"), A("test:regress_expected"), A("test:regress_run"))), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:setminus"), S3(A("CONS"), A("test:regress_run"), A("test:regress_expected")))), K(PHI()))),

DEF("test:regress_report",
    S5(A("CONS"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("regress-built")), A("test:regress_built")), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("regress-errors")), A("test:regress_errors")), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("regress-readback")), A("test:regress_readback")), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("regress-carriers")), A("test:regress_carriers")))),

"THE OBSERVED-FACTS LAW: A CASE SHOWS THE FACTS ITS OBSERVATIONS ARE OF, ONE SECTION PER FACT TYPE. The app is a case list whose churn case entities link the request logs, posthog recordings, communications, issues and commits; a churn case is a Sherlock Case whose Observations are of facts, by `Observation is of Fact`, and no reading is invented around Case. The fixture states its facts as sherlock does: Case c1 observes the Observations o1 and o2, o1 is of the Facts f1 and f2, o2 of f3, and o9, which c1 does not observe, of f9; each Fact is of its Fact Type and fills its Roles, and each RoleInstance, named `<fact>.<role>` as every objectified instance is, uses its Object Type Instance. f1 says Log Entry le1 has Call Outcome failed, f3 that le2 has not found, f2 that Cancel Request cr1 has PostHog Session Recording rec1, f9 that le9 failed. ui:facts of c1, its relation section aside, holds a section titled Log Entry has Call Outcome whose items link Log Entry le1 and le2, a section titled Cancel Request has PostHog Session Recording whose one item links Cancel Request cr1, and no item that links le9. The detail view showed the two Observations and stopped there, so the law answered F before the fix.",
DEF("test:held_store",
    K(S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S5(S2(A("CaseObservesObservation"), S2(A("Case"), A("Observation"))), S2(A("ObservationIsOfFact"), S2(A("Observation"), A("Fact"))), S2(A("FactIsOfFactType"), S2(A("Fact"), A("Fact Type"))), S2(A("LogEntryHasCallOutcome"), S2(A("Log Entry"), A("Call Outcome"))), S2(A("CancelRequestHasPostHogSessionRecording"), S2(A("Cancel Request"), A("PostHog Session Recording")))))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S5(
          S3(A("CELL"), A("ObservationIsOfFact"), S4(S2(A("o1"), A("f1")), S2(A("o1"), A("f2")), S2(A("o2"), A("f3")), S2(A("o9"), A("f9")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("FactIsOfFactType"), S4(S2(A("f1"), A("LogEntryHasCallOutcome")), S2(A("f2"), A("CancelRequestHasPostHogSessionRecording")), S2(A("f3"), A("LogEntryHasCallOutcome")), S2(A("f9"), A("LogEntryHasCallOutcome")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("RoleInstance"), S8(S2(A("f1"), A("LogEntryHasCallOutcome.1")), S2(A("f1"), A("LogEntryHasCallOutcome.2")), S2(A("f2"), A("CancelRequestHasPostHogSessionRecording.1")), S2(A("f2"), A("CancelRequestHasPostHogSessionRecording.2")), S2(A("f3"), A("LogEntryHasCallOutcome.1")), S2(A("f3"), A("LogEntryHasCallOutcome.2")), S2(A("f9"), A("LogEntryHasCallOutcome.1")), S2(A("f9"), A("LogEntryHasCallOutcome.2")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("RoleInstanceUsesObjectTypeInstance"), S8(S2(A("f1.LogEntryHasCallOutcome.1"), A("le1")), S2(A("f1.LogEntryHasCallOutcome.2"), A("failed")), S2(A("f2.CancelRequestHasPostHogSessionRecording.1"), A("cr1")), S2(A("f2.CancelRequestHasPostHogSessionRecording.2"), A("rec1")), S2(A("f3.LogEntryHasCallOutcome.1"), A("le2")), S2(A("f3.LogEntryHasCallOutcome.2"), A("not found")), S2(A("f9.LogEntryHasCallOutcome.1"), A("le9")), S2(A("f9.LogEntryHasCallOutcome.2"), A("failed")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("ObjectTypeIsSubtypeOfObjectType"), S1(S2(A("Support Request"), A("Request"))))))))),
"The context ui:facts takes for c1: <<descriptors, readings, tables, store>, table, id>, the one descriptor c1 plays in cut to its rows.",
DEF("test:held_view",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("ui:facts"), S4(A("CONS"),
       S5(A("CONS"),
          K(S1(S5(A("CaseObservesObservation"), S2(A("Case"), A("Observation")), S1(S1(N(2))), PHI(), S2(S2(A("c1"), A("o1")), S2(A("c1"), A("o2")))))),
          K(S3(S3(A("CaseObservesObservation"), S2(A("Case"), A("Observation")), S3(A("{0}"), A("observes"), A("{1}"))), S3(A("LogEntryHasCallOutcome"), S2(A("Log Entry"), A("Call Outcome")), S3(A("{0}"), A("has"), A("{1}"))), S3(A("CancelRequestHasPostHogSessionRecording"), S2(A("Cancel Request"), A("PostHog Session Recording")), S3(A("{0}"), A("has"), A("{1}"))))),
          K(S2(A("Case"), A("Observation"))),
          A("test:held_store")),
       K(A("Case")), K(A("c1"))))),
"Each section of test:held_view as <title, the links of its items>.",
DEF("test:held_pairs",
    S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), S3(A("CONS"), N(2), S4(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(4)), A("tl"), A("tl")))), A("test:held_view"))),
DEF("test:observed_facts",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("fp:and_all"), S4(A("CONS"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(S2(A("Log Entry has Call Outcome"), S2(S2(A("Log Entry"), A("le1")), S2(A("Log Entry"), A("le2"))))), A("test:held_pairs"))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(S2(A("Cancel Request has PostHog Session Recording"), S1(S2(A("Cancel Request"), A("cr1"))))), A("test:held_pairs"))),
       S4(A("COMP"), A("not"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(S2(A("Log Entry"), A("le9"))), S4(A("COMP"), A("theta:flatten"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(2)), A("test:held_pairs"))))))),

"THE NO-STATUS ACTIONS LAW: AN ENTITY THE MACHINE HOLDS AT NO STATUS IS OFFERED NO ACTIONS SECTION. The Case screen, routed as ui:route routes it, <store, <Case, id>, back, matches>, over support.auto.dev`s carriers, which hold its schema and none of its instances, threw `implode on sequence` in ui:transitions: the type has a machine, Investigation is for Case, so the section was drawn, and its title imploded ui:status, which answers nothing for an id no machine holds. ui:status_section already shows no status there, and with no status no transition is offered, so the section is none. The fixture is that machine, Investigation is for Case with open initial, and c9, an id the store holds nowhere. It threw before the fix.",
DEF("test:nostatus_store",
    K(S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S2(
       S3(A("CELL"), A("StateMachineDefinitionIsForObjectType"), S1(S2(A("Investigation"), A("Case")))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("StatusIsInitialInStateMachineDefinition"), S1(S2(A("open"), A("Investigation"))))))))),
DEF("test:nostatus_actions",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), S3(A("COMP"), A("ui:transitions"), S4(A("CONS"), A("test:nostatus_store"), K(A("Case")), K(A("c9")))))),

"THE ENTITY-COLUMNS LAW: on the composed store, the column section of Function render:listview holds its Definition Origin as the value registered, holds no Object Kind control, because render:listview is no Object Type, and the column section of Function Function does hold its Object Kind entity. Each column row is <field, label, fact type, type, options, value>, so the law reads the fact type and the value. It failed first when it was told the origin reads compiled.",
