"THE CASES ARE A LAW. Nothing heavy or long-running is part of the normal dev process, and every check adheres to the framework standards. The 768 cases of engine/shared/scenarios.canon, each an expression and its operand, and their answers in engine/shared/expected-cases.tsv, which every host holds its own answers to, were checked only by the JS host's test file. That file also spawns builds and stores, and it was reaped for memory before it finished. test:cases is the same check inside the report. Every case prints, as main:case_text prints it for the CLI, the answer recorded for it, and the two are compared encoded, as the file records them, by quote_str, which is JSON's string encoding (render:json would print an answer that reads as a number without its quotes). A recorded refusal is skipped, because a case that bottoms ends the report, and that is how a regression into one is seen. A line that is not a name and an answer is skipped too (the file has one blank). test:cases_off names the cases that answer otherwise. Operand: the store.",
